# Recent advances of two-dimensional metal-organic frameworks in alkaline electrolysis water for hydrogen production

Peisen Liao1,2, Jiawei Kang1, Yicheng Zhong1, Runan Xiang1, Shihan Wang1, Suisheng Li1, Xianlong Liu1 & Guangqin Li1
1 School of Chemistry, Sun Yat-sen University, Guangzhou 510275, China; 2 School of Chemistry and Environment, Jiaying University, Meizhou 514015, China
Hydrogen evolution production via electrolysis of water in alkaline solution is a promising mean of industrial hydrogen production. However, inefficiency and high cost restrict its industrialization development. Based on extensive studies of experimental and theoretical investigations, two-dimensional (2D) materials are promising substitute of noble metals for hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) with low cost and high-efficiency. Herein, recent progress in 2D metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) for HER in alkaline electrolyte is systematically discussed and summarized. This review describes the strategies about how to design and optimize the structure of 2D MOF-based materials as HER electrocatalysts with well performance and aims to provide potential avenues for industrial hydrogen production. Additionally, the challenges and future directions ahead in this field are also proposed.
## hydrogen production, 2D materials, electroctalyst, alkaline, metal-organic frameworks
Citation: Liao P, Kang J, Zhong Y, Xiang R, Wang S, Li S, Liu X, Li G. Recent advances of two-dimensional metal-organic frameworks in alkaline electrolysis water for hydrogen production. Sc China Chem, 2023, 66: 1924–1939, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-022-1545-0
## 1 Introduction
The continuous growth of the world population and economy is accompanied by a monumental increase in energy demand [1]. The trend of classic energy supply (e.g., fossil fuel) resources are depleted, accompanied by climate change and environmental deterioration [2]. Hydrogen is an attracting energy source due to its high specific energy density, broad resource of raw material, and only clean water as the combustion product [3–6]. According to the market research report, the global hydrogen market is expected to grow by $47\%$ from 142 billion USD in 2019 to 209 billion USD in 2027 [7]. However, over $95\%$ of hydrogen is made by steam methane reforming, resulting in releasing 830 million tons of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ annually [4,8]. Among various hydrogen production routes, green and sustainable hydrogen made from water electrolysis via hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) is a promising way for high-purity $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production as the price of renewable electricity continues to plummet [9,10].
It has been a long history of water electrolysis since its first discovery. As shown in Figure 1, in 1789, Paets van Troostwijk and Deiman discovered electrolytic water decomposition into hydrogen and oxygen on gold electrode for the first time [11]. The voltaic pile was invented by Alessandro Volta in 1800 [12]. After 33 years later, Faraday’s Law was proposed and the definition of water electrolysis was then formalized [13]. In 1869, the invention of Gramme machine made electrolysis of water become a cheap method [14]. After a period of development, the industrialization of hydrogen production through alkaline water electrolysis was achieved for the first time in 1888 [15]. And then water electrolysis entered a stage of rapid development. In 1890, commercialized separator asbestos membrane was developed [16]. In 1939, the first large water electrolysis electrolyzer with a capacity of $10,\bar{0}00\ \mathrm{~N~m~}^{3}\ \mathrm{~H}_{2}\ \mathrm{~h}^{-1}$ was in operation. In 1948, Zdansky/Lonza built the first pressurized industrial electrolyzer [12]. Proton exchange membrane (PEM) was first used in water electrolysis by General Electric in 1966 [17]. With the development of the Times, many new technologies and new materials emerge endlessly, alkaline electrolytic water faces both opportunities and challenges. Its inefficiency and high cost make it not yet competitive with existing hydrogen production methods. However, the fast development of renewable power will lower the cost of water electrolysis. The combination of traditional water electrolysis with new materials may bring about new breakthroughs. Specially, a new class of porous materials metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with unique structural features, high surface areas, chemical tunability and stability was discovered in 1990s, but until 2007, the electrochemistry of MOFs was first studied [18]. In 2009, Professor Hiroshi Kitagawa’s research group [19] conducted the first case of conductive MOFs. The first industrial MOF synthesis and commercial MOF product was achieved in 2010 and 2016, respectively [20]. An early case of MOF towards HER was reported by Kian Ping Loh’s group in 2013 [21]. Now MOFs have become star materials in the field of electrocatalytic energy [22–25], and hydrogen production at Abe level using MOFs as electrocatalyst has been achieved [26].
Water electrolysis for hydrogen production can be improved by optimizing elecatalysts and electrolytes. And the latter can be divided into acidic/alkaline/neutral electrolytes (e.g., ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ , KOH or phosphate buffer solution) according to the $\mathsf{p H}$ value. Catalyst in acidic electrolytes exhibited superior activity towards HER due to the high concentration of protons/hydroniums as reactants [27,28]. However, proton exchange membrane (PEM)-based acid electrolytic cell normally operating at high temperatures inevitably leads to corrosive electrolyte and acidic fogs coming up when working in acidic electrolytes, which contaminate the produced $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and corrode electrolyzers [29,30]. Producing hydrogen from water under neutral conditions is inherently tough because of the low conductivity and poor activity, which is still in the laboratory stage [31,32]. Hydrogen production by alkaline electrolytic cell is the most mature technology in industry with the longest developed technical means. This method has the advantages of simple equipment and convenient operation, which makes it widely developed [33]. The alkaline electrolyzer has lower vapor pressure and does not cause as much corrosion so high purity $(>99.999\%)$ hydrogen can be generated [34]. Moreover, non-noble metal electrocatalysts, which maintain relative stability and avoid corrosion and dissolution, show greater potential in alkaline media, but further improvements are needed [35,36]. Therefore, relative to the other two methods, alkaline electrolysis is a more attractive alternative for HER [37–39].
As mentioned below, the HER under alkaline condition needs additional water dissociation step making it more challenging compared to the acid electrolyte. Pt-group metals are the most exceptional catalysts, which can meet the current densities in the practical industrial application [40,41]. However, the limited resource and high cost of these noble metals prohibit the application of noble metals in industrial scale [29,42]. More efficent and low cost electrocatalysts are demanded.
In particular, two-dimensional (2D) materials have attracted significant research attention due to their unique structures and excellent physical properties [43]. Many 2D materials (Figure 2), including 2D MOFs, 2D covalent organic frameworks (COFs), transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDCs), layered double hydroxides (LDHs), 2D Mexenes, and 2D graphene, can be exfoliated into single or few layers in thickness on account of strong in-plane chemical bonds and weak interlayer interaction [44–46]. Moreover, the layered materials could provide ultimate atom-utilization efficiency and excellent conductivity due to the high specific surface area and abundant surface defects [47,48]. Thus 2D materials are promising candidates to replace noble metals for HER with low cost and high efficency [47,49]. Importantly, 2D materials are ideal investigated models of computational prediction with experimental confirmation conveniently [50–52]. Compared with other 2D materials, 2D MOFs constructed from metal ions and organic ligands, have the following advantages: (1) great varieties–the diversity of metal and ligand combination results in thousands of MOFs; (2) designability–the structure of 2D MOFs are easy to design and customize according to the function; (3) rich host-guest properties–due to the unique structure, abundant pores exit in the 2D MOFs providing space for interacting with chemical species. Host-guest interaction, including electrostatic interaction, ion exchange, and $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking, can be used to encapsulate metal nanoparticles or catalytic molecules.
In this review, we mainly focus on the recent progress oin HER in alkaline media based on 2D MOFs. Firstly, the mechanisms of HER in acid, alkaline and neutral media are described. Then some effective strategies for enhancing the performance of HER in alkaline are discussed in classification. Finally, the opportunities and challenges of 2D MOFbased electrocatalysts in basic media for HER to meet the commercial level are also proposed. This review aims to summarize the existing 2D MOFs with outstanding performance towards HER in alkaline media, providing new avenues based on 2D MOFs for the development of industrial hydrogen production.
## 2 Mechanism of HER
As the HER is the cathode half-reaction in overall splitting water, the other half-reaction is oxygen evolution reaction (OER) at anode. The overall reaction is carried out as follow:
$$
\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}{\rightarrow}\mathrm{H}_{2}{+}1/2\mathrm{O}_{2}
$$
Volmer step:
$$
\mathrm{H}^{+}+\mathrm{e}^{-}+^{\ast}\rightarrow\mathrm{H}^{\ast}
$$
$$
\mathrm{H}^{+}+\mathrm{e}^{-}+\mathrm{H}^{\ast}\rightarrow\mathrm{H}_{2}
$$
Tafel step:
$$
2\mathrm{H}^{*}\rightarrow\mathrm{H}_{2}+2^{*}
$$
Volmer step:
$$
\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}+\mathrm{e}^{-}+^{*}\xrightarrow{}\mathrm{H}^{*}+\mathrm{OH}^{-}
$$
$$
\Pi_{2}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{O}}+\mathbf{e}^{-}+\mathbf{H}^{*}\rightarrow\mathbf{H}_{2}+\boldsymbol{\mathrm{OH}}^{-}
$$
Tafel step:
$$
2\mathrm{H}^{*}\rightarrow\mathrm{H}_{2}+2^{*}
$$
$\eta=a+b\log j$ (8) where $\eta$ is the overpotential, $b$ is the Tafel slope, and $j$ is the current density. Besides, the most commonly used parameter is the overpotential to reach a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ which has real industrial value.
## 3 2D MOF for hydrogen production in alkaline solution
2D MOFs have been emerged as efficient electrocatalysts due to its characteristics, facilitating active sites loading, and intermediates adsorption [64–68]. Compared with their 3D forms, 2D MOFs possess ultrathin layer thickness, abundant exposed unsaturated metal sites, higher surface-to-volume ratios, and weak interlayer force. These characteristics make it different from 3D MOFs when designed as HER electrocatalyst. For 2D MOFs, it concerns more about how to optimize exposed sites reacting with substrates effectively. Moreover, due to the interlayer force, highly efficient electocatalyst can be constructed from other 2D materials/molecules and 2D MOFs, synergically promoting HER reaction. For 3D MOFs, more catalytic sites need to be exposed when they used as electrocatalysts. The synthesis methods of 2D MOF have been detailed in previous reports [45,69,70]. To form 2D MOFs, the ligands should serve to restrict out of plane growth, such as phthalocyanine, porphyrin, and benzene-derived ligands with high density of electrons and $\pi$ - bonds, and have 2, 3, or 4 coordination sites to the metal linker. Moreover, functional groups of the ligands, which should be short bridging, such as cyanides, azides, and nitrile donors, can provide predictable binding modes that prevent out of plane rotation. It is encouraging to form planar geometry combinations when choosing ligands and metal ions. Fourfold coordination symmetry is the mostly form, resulting in a square planar structure [71]. The review here mainly focuses on how to design electrocatalyst from the structural composition of 2D MOFs to improve the kinetics of charge transfer for HER in alkaline solution. For pristine MOFs, conductive ligand design, metal node regulation, interface engineering and host-guest effects are efficient tools, while for derivative MOFs, annealing process and the substance cooperated with MOF during pyrolysis make a great influence on HER process.
## 3.1 Pristine 2D MOF
Pristine 2D MOFs are crystalline porous materials with ultrathin thickness, but abundant intrinsic molecular metal sites, poor electrical conductivity, limited mass transfer, and organic ligand covering active metal center, still restrict the application of MOFs for electrocatalysis hydrogen production in alkaline solution [72–76]. To solve these problems, great efforts have been made. Based on the structural characteristics and properties of MOFs, four strategies for improving HER activity are shown below.
## 3.1.1 Conductive ligands design
Most of pristine MOFs are nonconducting due to the poor charge mobility of organic ligands [77–79]. Thus constructing conductive MOFs from metal nodes, which have electrons with high energy (e.g., ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ ) and redox active linkers, can be efficient for improving HER activity [52,80– 83]. For example, Feng’s group [84] used the 1,2,5,6,9,10- triphenylenehexathiol (THT) and nickel salts to synthesize a kind of large-area, free-standing 2D MOF single-layer sheet, which exhibited a good performance on HER, with an overpotential of $574~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ in 0.05 M KOH. Moreover, Chen’s group [85] reported a bimetallic sited conductive 2D MOF with an extra $\mathbf{M}{\cdot}\mathbf{N}_{2}$ moiety prepared by conjugated ligand hexaiminohexaazatrinaphthalene (HAHATN, also denoted CAT). The metal atom of extra $\mathbf{M}{\cdot}\mathbf{N}_{2}$ moiety possessed a higher unsaturation site than that at the $\mathrm{\mathbf{M}\mathrm{-}N_{4}}$ , leading to stronger electroactivity. $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{Ni}_{3}$ · $\mathrm{HAHATN})_{2}$ nanosheets were active for catalyzing HER with a small overpotential of $115\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , a low Tafel slope of $45.6~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1}$ and promising electrocatalytic stability (Figure 4). Additionally, Hou and co-workers [26] developed a sulfur-ligand introducing strategy to build wellaligned Ni-benzenedicarboxylic acid (BDC)-based MOF nanosheet arrays, which exhibits a low overpotential of 310 $\mathrm{mV}$ to attain $1.0\mathrm{A}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . However, though conductive MOFs can effectively improve the intrinsic HER activity of materials, the conductive ligand design approach is still in its infancy due to the limited types.
## 3.1.2 Metal node regulation
Metal nodes are important part in the MOFs, and they act not only as the structural composition, but also as catalytic centers. Their electronic states and chemical environment determine the HER catalytic performance. For example, metal centers of MOFs in different halogen environments [86], or coordinated with modified ligands [87] exhibit significantly different properties. It has been found that 2D MOFs have excellent catalytic activity by node regulation to expose more activation sites, regulate electronic structure, and optimize the adsorption energy of intermediate in HER.
Furthermore, etching strategy can be employed to create more defect sites, regulating the coordination environment of metal nodes. For example, Li’s group [99] applied a convenient alkali-etched strategy to break the Ni–O bonds of 2D $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})$ nanosheet arrays (Figure 6), generating more unsaturated $\mathrm{\DeltaNi}$ sites and introducing the extra-framework K cations which enhanced charge transfer capability. The overpotential of defect-Ni-MOF was $101~\mathrm{\mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for HER in $1\mathrm{MKOH}$ , visibly smaller than that of Ni-MOF nanosheet array $(232~\mathrm{mV})$ and $\mathrm{Ni(OH)}_{2}$ nanosheet arrays $\mathrm{282~mV)}$ . Specially, Wang and Gong et al. [100] used the MOF-etching strategy to fabricate Mo-CoP/ NC/TF HER catalyst with small overpotentials of $78~\mathrm{mV}$ at current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ in alkaline electrolytes. The DFT results proved that the synergistic effect between MoCoP doping and N-carbon doping could effectively regulate the electronic structure, thus promoting the thermal neutral adsorption and interfacial charge transfer kinetics of hydrogen intermediates. Similarly, other researches about etching strategy to regulate metal nodes of MOFs towards HER in alkaline electrolytes had been investigated, such as constructing CoP nanostructure arrays on nickel foam substrate from 2D cobalt-based MOF [101], or fabricating selfsupported NiCoZnP/NC nanosheets array on carbon cloth (CC) [102].
## 3.1.3 Interfaces engineering
Water electrolysis is a heterogeneous reaction, where hydrogen production occurs at the solid-liquid interface, mass transport and charge transfer directly affect the catalytic efficiency, and thus the interface design of catalyst is crucial adjective. Numerous in-depth studies have shown that interface engineering is of critical significance for improving the performance of MOF electrocatalysts particularly towards HER [103,104]. Interface engineering to modified 2D MOFs can initiated by the interfacial material composition (metal, ligand, oxide, nitride, etc.), interfacial structure (including heterojunction, interpenetration, stacking, and other connection modes) from different interfacial dimensions (including 1D, 2D, and 3D) to explore the internal relationship between interfacial electronic transmission and HER catalytic performance. For example, for 2D MOFs growing on 1D substrate (such as carbon fiber and metal nanorods), 2D carrier (such as graphene) or 3D substrate (such as carbon felt-cloth), the interfacial electronic transmission between 2D MOFs and substrate will be interesting, since the direction of the electron transfer is different in different dimension substrates.
(1) Interfacial material composition
The incorporation of 2D MOFs with other materials can effectively enhance electron transport and increase the active sites, thereby promoting the HER electrocatalytic activity. It is effective to design well-defined interfacial chemical bond bridges between MOFs and other 2D materials. For example, through interface effect of MOFs, the phase transition of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ can be achieved. Qiao’s group [105] developed a novel $2\mathrm{D}\mathrm{Co-BDC/MoS}_{2}$ hybrid nanosheets via a facile sonicationassisted solution strategy. A partial phase transfer from semiconducting $2\mathrm{H}{\cdot}\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ to metallic $\mathrm{1T-MoS}_{2}$ occured when 2D Co-BDC was induced. More catalytic active sites in $\mathrm{Co-BDC}/1\mathrm{T-MoS}_{2}$ makes Co-BDC speed up the sluggish water dissociation in alkaline media, promotes the generation and desorption of hydrogen on $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ , and results in a significant performance improvement of HER activity, outperforming those of each component. Another interesting example was reported by Han’s group [106], where the shape of graphene oxide (GO)-modified zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF) was adjusted by the trapping of water molecules into the interlayers of GO from typical leaf-like to elongated hexagonal shape (Figure 7). Attributed to the unique shape features, the leaf-like catalyst showed better HER activity. Moreover, Zhu’s group [107] reported that 2D black phosphorus (BP) was used as template to grow 2D MOF- $.\mathrm{Fe}/\$ Co forming BP@MOF heterojunction with strong interactions. The catalyst exhibits high HER activity in $1\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{KOH}$ . Semimetal 2D MXenes, exhibits efficient charge transport and rich surface chemistry, which enables them to form tightly connected 2D/2D heterostructures when coupling with Co-BDC nanosheets, reported by Peng and co-workers [108]. As verified by characterization, the $_\mathrm{Co-O-Ti}$ bond bridging forming at the interface enhances the HER reaction kinetics.
## (2) Interfacial structure
Moreover, the interface effect facilitates the formation of hierarchical channels to improve the effective surface area and electric conductivity, thereby optimizing the kinetics of HER. For example, Mousavi and co-workers [112] realized a layer-by-layer assembled trimetallic Fe–Co–Ni porous MOF in which the metal cations within each layer or at the interface of the two layers are linked to one another by bridging 2- amino-BDC linkers to tailoring catalytically active sites for HER. Qiu’s group [113] developed a high-efficiency electrocatalyst with hierarchical $\mathrm{(Cu_{3}(P O_{4})_{2}/C u}$ -BDC) nanosheets array supported by copper foam. Copper ions released from the substrate, which was facile and controllable. The optimal electrocatalyst $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{2}/\mathrm{Cu}$ -BDC150-6 foam exhibited excellent electrocatalytic performance for HER in $1\mathrm{M}\mathrm{KOH}$ ( $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ at overpotential of 145 $\mathrm{mV}$ ) with excellent catalytic stability. When using nickel foam as substrate, Co-MOF nanosheet arrays on nickel foam were obtained by a CoO nanowall template-induced strategy reported by Wang’s group [114].
## 3.1.4 Host-guest effects
The synergistic effect, electronic interaction, interfacial bonding, and lattice strain from different components in porous heterostructured 2D MOFs electrocatalysts, would appropriately optimize the binding energy of targeted intermediates to boost kinetics of HER [33,115].
Phthalocyanine $(\mathrm{Pc})$ , porphyrin, and other macrocyclic conjugated molecules have strong host-guest interaction with 2D MOFs, which can be introduced into the MOF layer to improve charge transport. For instance, Chen’s group [121] developed a 2D MOF-supported FePc composite nanosheets based on Ni-BDC towards HER electrocatalysis. As show in the Figure 10, on account of the ultralarge specific surface of Ni-MOF, well dispersed organic molecules and strong $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions between FePc and 2D MOFs, the composite nanosheets show a substantially increased current density, lowest overpotential $334\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and enhanced durability $(10\mathrm{h})$ compared with FePc, Ni-MOF, and the counterpart FePc@bulk-MOF.
## 3.2 Derivative of 2D MOFs
In addition, to further improve the conductivity and stability, the organic ligands of 2D MOFs can be graphitized through a certain degree of annealing treatment, while the frame still maintains well, and more active sites can be exposed [122]. Benefitting from the unique superiorities of high electrochemical active surface areas, optimized electronic structure, and enhanced electric conductivity, the electrocatalysts derived from rationally designed 2D MOFs exhibit expected performance. MOFs as precursor to synthesize electrocatalysts have attracted much attention due to their synergistic effect and well-defined porous nanostructure [123,124].
3.2.1 Annealing atmosphere regulation Since MOFs derivatives are obtained by annealing on the basis of MOFs, the annealing processes and parameters, especially the gas atmosphere are particularly important.
First of all, a large part of them are operated in an inert atmosphere to avoid to introducing new impurities and to maintain the original frame structure as much as possible. For example, Wu and co-workers [125] reported a novel cation modulating metal carbide electrocatalyst $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}Z\mathrm{nC}_{0.7}$ by thermal annealing from homogeneous bimetallic NiZnMOFs. The balance of $\mathrm{Ni}(0)$ and Ni(II) in $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}Z\mathrm{nC}_{0.7}$ through Zn modulation is beneficial to water splitting. Furthermore, attributed to the cation modulation and the unique nanostructured derived from MOF precursor, $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}Z\mathrm{nC}_{0.7}$ presents an overpotential of $1.65\mathrm{V}$ with a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{\cim}^{-2}$ for overall water splitting, which is comparable to commercial $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Ru}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ counterparts. Another ternary metal composition Ru-doped cobalt-nickel oxide heterostructure growing on $\mathrm{Ni}$ foam $\mathrm{{Ru-Co}}_{3}\mathrm{{O}}_{4}.$ -NiO-NF was fabricated by Song’s group under Ar atmosphere (Figure 11). The tailored low-temperature annealing process made Ru$\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}\mathrm{-NiO-NF}}$ inherit the advantageous 2D nanosheet morphology of Co-MOF and the mechanical robustness of 3D Ni foam, which were beneficial for HER activity [126].
Secondly, it was found that nitrogen-group element compounds derived from 2D-MOFs demonstrate superior HER performance. The annealing process under N-containing atmosphere (e.g., $\Nu_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{.}$ ) may induce nitrogen atom into the derivative of MOF. For instance, Li and co-workers [127] reported that the pyrolysis of $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}\mathrm{TED}$ under $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ would take off the redundant carbon and promote surface nitridation (Figure 12). The Ni- ${\bf-0.2N H}_{3}$ ( ${\mathrm{0.2NH}_{3}}$ means $20\%$ $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ in Ar) coated by an ultrathin carbon layer showed the best performance. They further optimized the condition and found that the chemical etching effect of $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ is crucial to attenuate the carbon coating layer [128]. The CoNiN nanoplates on amorphous carbon network synthesized by Lee’s group [129] under $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ atmosphere demonstrated great improvement for HER, where metal nitrides played a great role. Hexamethylenetetramine [130], triethylenediamine or even $\Nu_{2}$ [131] can also be as $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ sources for N-doped MOF derivative.
Thirdly, numerous MOF-derived phosphating methods have been developed due to the potential of transition metal phosphides to improve the electrocatalytic performance for their metalloid characteristics and excellent electrical conductivity. For example, Xu’s group [132] designed a porous 3D interconnected network with well-arranged 2D carbon nanosheets on it, in which ultrafine cobalt phosphide nanoparticles $(3{-}4\mathrm{nm})$ were tightly immobilized via carbonization/phosphidation pyrolysis. The catalyst showed excellent HER activity with an overpotential of $91~\mathrm{mV}$ to achieve $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The mechanism study shows that N-containing 2D Co-MOF could greatly restrict the reunion of metal nanoparticles, which as basic coordination sites contribute to produce the resultant metal phosphides in small size, with excellent transport properties of mass/electrons. In 2019, by employing facile liquid-phase reactions and subsequently phosphorization process from a Co-based MOFs precursor, Wang et al. [133] fabricated a hierarchically porous W-doped CoP nanoflake arrays (W-CoP NAs/CC) as a binder-free catalyst for HER in pH-universal electrolytes. With the rich exposed active sites and accelerated diffusion of electrolytes and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ bubbles, W-CoP NAs/CC possesses efficient HER activity. Moreover, the strong electron interactions between W and CoP by W doping can modulate the electronic states of CoP to enhance the HER performance. Importantly, WCoP NAs/CC delivers a nearly $100\%$ Faradaic efficiency and a low overpotential of $94~\mathrm{mV}$ in alkaline electrolytes at a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . The above examples are derived from ${\mathrm{NaH}}_{2}{\mathrm{PO}}_{2}$ as a phosphorus source, which prones to disproportionation reaction to produce metal phosphates. Furthermore, it is easy to obtain P-doped 2D3D-hybrid porous MOF electrocatalysts with unique architecture [134], such as triangular nanowall assembled by 3D microsphere [135], unique flytrap-like biomimetic morphology [136], nanosheets [137,138], or 2D NiCoP Nanoflakes [139] derived from MOF and LDH nanosheets for efficient HER at high current densities. Moreover, Zheng and co-workers found nanocarbon $(\mathrm{CoP}@{\mathrm{C}})$ from 2D Co-MOF by low-temperature phosphorization exhibited superior HER activity compared to 3D structure Co-MOF [140]. Similarly, Wang and co-workers [141] developed an $\mathrm{Ru-Co_{2}P/N-C/N F}$ derived from MOF templates, which showed excellent bifunctional catalytic performance for HER in alkaline media. The process of transforming CoRu-MOF to ${\mathrm{Ru-Co}}_{2}{\mathrm{P}}$ nanoparticles could modulate the electronic structure of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ with Ru, and accelerate the charge transfer.
Fourthly, interestingly, the derivation of MOF in the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ atmosphere may produce unexpected surprises. Lai and coworkers [142] reported a novel ${\mathrm{Co-MOF/H}}_{2}$ catalyst by annealing of 2D Co-MOF which was confined in the inner cavity of CNTs under $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ atmosphere. $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ as reductant makes Co-MOF fast turn into $\mathrm{Co/CoO}_{x}$ nanoparticles and catalyzes the growth of CNTs from MOF as carbon source. Moreover, Geometrical phase analysis (GPA) has shown the introduction of lattice tensile strain in the $\mathrm{Co/CoO}_{x}$ crystal. The resultant ${\mathrm{Co-MOF/H}}_{2}$ has 2–3 graphitic layers and presents abundant high valent ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , leading to efficient reactant adsorption and charge transfer, and resulting in high HER activity which requires an overpotential of only $30\mathrm{mV}$ to reach a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ in $1\ \mathrm{M\KOH}$ .
## 3.2.2 Cooperated annealing strategy
As mentioned above, varies of guest molecules or nanoparticles can be encapsulated into 2D MOFs based on the host-guest interaction. During the annealing process, guest molecules will release or carbonize with organic ligands in the MOFs, leading to hierarchical pore structures. Different components (e.g., metal nanoparticles/C, metal alloy, and metal oxides/C) can be obtained according to the composition of 2D MOFs and guest molecules. On this basis, many MOF-derived methods have been developed to fabricate effective HER catalysts with excellent electroactivity and clear catalytic mechanism.
## (1) Metal nanoparticles/C
Usually, metal nanoparticles/C formed in the MOFs with single metal nodes. For example, Xu’s group [143] reported Nickel nanoparticles encapsulated in few-layer nitrogendoped graphene $(\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{NC})$ which are derived from 2D Nibased MOFs via high-temperature annealing treatment. The electrocatalytic performance of $\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{NCs}$ materials can be tuned by changing annealing temperature. There are synergistic effects between Ni NP core and the N-doped thin graphene shell $(0.34\mathrm{nm})$ . The resulting $\mathsf{N i}@\mathsf{N C}$ materials exhibit highly efficient and ultrastable electrocatalytic activity toward the HER (a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at a low overpotential of $205~\mathrm{mV},$ and overall water splitting (applied potential of $1.60\mathrm{V},$ ) in alkaline media. Moreover, additional carbon sources such as graphene oxide with 2D structure can enrich the structural types of MOF-derived materials. As shown in the Figure 13, Wang and co-workers [144] reported 1D carbon nanotubes formed from the 2D MOF linking with the 2D reduced GO sheets to stitch the 3D freestanding film. It was found that Ni NP cores and N-CNT shells were strongly coupled as hexatomic carbon rings sourcing from organic ligands in MOFs because the basic units of CNTs can be used directly to decrease the cyclization energy barriers, enabling the easy formation of N-CNT from the inside to the outside on the surface. Specially, the incorporation of a small amount of second metal ions can also facilitate the formation of metal nanoparticles through cooperated annealing strategy. Sun and co-workers [145] proposed a novel Pt NPs encapsulated in ultrathin 2D carbon layers derived from the ultrathin 2D MOF precursor (ZIF67). The thickness of the ultrathin 2D MOFs was controlled through a step-by-step method. 2D carbon layers suppressed the growth of Pt NPs and stabilized it with enriched active sites ${\mathrm{Co-N}}_{x}$ . Electrochemical tests revealed the resultant materials with highly dispersed active sites boost Pt activities toward HER with a lower overpotential $37.2\mathrm{mV}$ in alkaline media) to reach the current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , even better than that of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalysts. As first principles simulations reveal, the enhanced electrochemical activities are attributed to the synergistic effect of $\mathrm{Pt}$ -based NPs and derived ultrathin 2D carbon layers.
## (2) Metal alloy/C
If the ratio of second metal ions is increased or the distance of two metal atoms and lattice parameter are appropriate, as mentioned above, by inducing metal ions through metal nodes regulation strategy, followed by pyrolyzation, metal alloy nanoparticles supported on carbon network would be easily obtained (Figure 14). For example, Lin and coworkers [131] reported a CoIr alloy supported on 2D graphitic-N doped carbon $\operatorname{CoIr}(\varnothing\operatorname{CN})$ with $2.69\mathrm{wt\%}$ Ir loading derived from Ir-doped 2D CoMOF, which exhibits a remarkably electrocatalytic activity for HER.
(3) Metal chalcogenide/C
Since most of the organic ligands in MOF are carboxylic acid ligands, it is easy to form metal oxides during annealing. Moreover, the oxygen-group element compounds, such as oxides, sulfides, and selenides derived from 2D MOFs, are considered as candidates to replace noble metal for efficient HER electrocatalysts.
(i) Metal oxides. In 2020, Yang’s group [146] reported a unique $\mathrm{FeNi_{3}–F e_{3}O_{4}}$ NPs/MOF-CNT electrocatalyst through hydrothermal reaction and subsequent partial decomposition of $\mathrm{Ni/Fe/C}$ MOF precursor. Benefiting from the unique porous nano architecture and the interaction between the interfaces (MOF with $\mathrm{FeNi}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , and CNT), $\mathrm{FeNi}_{3}–\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs/MOF-CNT displayed excellent performance toward overall water splitting. When employed as bifunctional electrocatalyst, at a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $\mathrm{FeNi}_{3}$ - $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs/MOF-CNT only requires a low overpotential of $108~\mathrm{mV}$ for HER, which outperforms the precious-metalbased electrocatalysts. Additionally, an effective 2D bifunctional catalyst, CoO-modified nitrogen and sulfur codoped ultrathin porous carbon nanoplates, was rationally designed and synthesized by pyrolysis of ultrathin $[{\mathrm{Co}}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{Tdc})].$ - $(4,4^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{Bpy})]_{n}$ (Tdc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ thiophene-2,5dicarboxylate; $\mathrm{Bpy}=4{,}4^{\prime}$ - bipyridine) nanoplates under nitrogen atmosphere (denoted as CoO/N-S-UPCNPs-600). The prepared materials exhibit high catalytic activity, stability, abundant defects, and large specific surface area [147].
(ii) Metal sulfides. Theoretically, metal sulfides are comparable to Pt because of the suitable hydrogen adsorption free energy, and are considered as substitutes for precious metals to catalyze HER [148]. Sulfur powder are the most commonly used S source cooperated with 2D MOFs forming into metal sulfides during annealing process. For instance, a free-standing and hierarchical hollow $\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\mathrm{-MoS}_{2}$ heteronanosheet array was prepared by an MOF-etching strategy with $\mathrm{MoO}_{4}^{2-}$ and in-situ sulfuration. Resulting from the controllable etching of 2D MOF, the obtained ${\mathrm{CoS}}_{2}{\mathrm{-MoS}}_{2}$ possesses abundant heterointerfaces with modulated local charge distribution, which promotes water dissociation and rapid electrocatalytic kinetics. Thus, the engineered $\mathrm{CoS}_{2}$ - $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ heterostructure exhibits a small overpotentials of 82 $\mathrm{mV}$ for HER at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which may provide valuable insights for developing MOF-derived heterogeneous electrocatalysts with tailored interface/surface structure [149]. Similarly, Kim’s group [150] developed a 2D MOF-derived mesoporous $\mathrm{CoS}_{2}$ nanoarrays coupled with $\mathrm{FeS}_{2}@\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ layers through sulfuration. Song’s group [151] reported a series of defect-rich Fe-doped $\mathrm{NiS}/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ ultrathin nanosheets. Besides molybdenum sulfide, cobalt sulfide and nickel sulfide are also widely concerned because of the high conductivity and inherent metal properties. A pod-like coupling structure of 3D Co-MOF nanocubes with 2D Co-MOF nanosheets $\mathrm{(2D/3D{\cdot}C o S_{2}@C C)}$ and a multicomponent $\mathrm{Ni/}$ $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{2}@\mathrm{CN}$ with unique pore structure derived from MOF precursor were prepared by Fu’s group [152] and Su’s group [153], respectively.
(iii) Metal selenide. Furthermore, a porous NiCoSe nanosheet arrays on Ni foam was prepared through a simple ion-exchange reaction followed by a hydrothermal process from 2D MOFs. Benefiting from the unique 2D multi-porous framework with high electrochemical active surface area, conductivity and open channels for the release of gaseous products, the as-prepared NiCoSe nanosheet arrays exhibit excellent electrochemical activity with a low overpotential of $170~\mathrm{mV}$ for HER at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ [154].
## 3.2.3 Combination of multiple strategies
Sometimes single strategy is not ideal and a combination of multiple strategies to achieve better HER activity may be efficient. Recently, $\mathrm{Co/Co98}$ core-shell structures anchored onto S, N co-doped porous graphene sheets $\mathrm{(Co/Co_{9}S_{8}}@\mathrm{\Omega}$ SNGS) were synthesized from the pyrolysis of dual organic ligands assembled $[\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{Tdc})(\mathrm{Bpy})]_{n}$ MOFs by Zhao’s group (Figure 15) [155]. The dual organic ligands in MOF provided S, N doping sources for the core-shell structures in the pyrolysis process. Doped N atoms could enhance the adsorption of H atoms, while S atoms could increase the number of active centers, and all of them would improve electronic transmission process. The obtained material $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\$ SNGS-1000 possessed a high surface area of $249.6\ensuremath{\mathrm{m}}^{2}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ and showed porous structure with a large pore volume of $0.235\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . For water splitting, $\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\mathrm{SNGS}{-}1000$ presents superior bifunctional catalytic activities in $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH electrolyte for both OER and HER, and only needs 290 $\mathrm{mV}$ to afford a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for OER and $350~\mathrm{mV}$ to reach a current density of $20\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for HER (Table 1). Additionally, innovative combination of doping, annealing, and sulfidation is developed by Hu and coworkers to fabricate hierarchical porous bimetal-sulfide bifunctional nanocatalysts for hydrogen production in alkaline solution [156].
## 4 Conclusions and perspective
In summary, 2D MOFs have been systematically discussed about their design strategy as promising candidates for electrochemical HER in alkaline media. This review intends to provide guidance for the future work related to the design of HER catalyst systems by in-depth profiling of the existing electrocatalyst based on 2D MOFs. Generally, two main points are proposed to improve their intrinsic catalytic activity in alkaline solution: (1) one is improving electrical conductivity to enhance the kinetics of charge transfer including ligands design, interfaces engineering, and annealing derivation; (2) the other is optimizing the intrinsic activity of catalytic sites, including nodes regulations, host-guest effects, and doping heteroatoms. Faster charge transfers and more active sites will lead to fast dissociation of water molecules on the surface of catalysts and quick forming of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules, resulting in superior performance in alkaline media.
However, though great progress has been made for HER in basic electrolytes, there remain certain challenges for 2D MOFs that call for in-depth studies: (1) HER electrocatalyst is still suffering from the corrosion during electrolysis, especially in heavy current to meet industrial $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production. (2) The HER mechanisms about the actual active sites for these 2D MOF catalysts are still unclear and need to be definitized. Combining theories together with experiment is a promising approach, but the modeling is challenging especially when it comes to complex systems. Nevertheless, we believe that the mechanisms will be clear with the development of advanced technologies (e.g., in situ XRD, XANES, operando Raman spectroscopy, and Frozen electron microscopy). In the future, researchers will increaseingly turn to in situ characterization for observing the HER reaction process and optimizing catalyst design, thus further advancing the development of the hydrogen economy. (3) Unified evaluation criteria for HER performance needs to be further refined. Nowadays testing conditions are varied, and electrochemical workstations, electrolytes, electrodes, the distinct loading mass of catalysts and some other parameters make reported result difficult to repeat. Third-party testing is necessary but difficult to execute. (4) The cost of catalysts needs to be considered when designing catalysts. Because the original purpose of hydrogen production is to alleviate the energy crisis and environmental pollution, green, low cost, and simple methods for synthesizing catalysts are worth advocating. Furthermore, emerging artificial intelligence may help us design new catalysts based on structure-function relationship. Given the commercial realities, we still have a long way to go for green efficient hydrogen production, but the progress we have made will give us the confidence and opportunity to move towards the ultimate goal.
